Severity scale: critical = permits false green or shared-state corruption; high = required outcome cannot be met as planned; medium = verification can certify the wrong cause; low = bounded defect.

[1] critical: false-green    loop/items/AI4DEV-81/plan.md:83
    claim: D4 treats syntactically local connection strings as positive provenance even though they are not bound to the runner’s validated slot evidence.
    why it matters: `localStackProblems` only checks loopback URLs, ports, and unsigned JWT claims (`tests/at/harness/runner.ts:714`), while the child receives four plain strings (`tests/at/harness/db-pool.ts:1249`); fabricated URLs and tokens with matching `iss`/`role` claims can therefore mint `real` without any prepared database answering. Step 3 tests malformed and absent evidence, but not valid-looking fabricated evidence or an opaque runner-issued attestation.
    unverified-runtime-claim: no

[2] critical: false-green    loop/items/AI4DEV-81/plan.md:90
    claim: The proposed generic live-backed route does not preserve the closed-table doctrine for `sut.*` names.
    why it matters: the current prefix route is considered safe specifically because it always grants `stand-in` (`tests/at/harness/capabilities.ts:304`); letting the same open-ended namespace grant `real` allows a new or misspelled `sut.fake` capability to bypass name-specific review. Step 3 only proves that the existing witness table refuses unknown names, not that the new live route does.
    unverified-runtime-claim: no

[3] high: unexecutable    tests/at/harness/index.ts:181
    claim: Every integration harness will still contain the `vendors.email` stand-in, so D2 rejects every planned green body before it reaches the live adapter.
    why it matters: `buildCapabilityLedger` always creates `EmailProviderSim` and registers it as `vendors.email` (`tests/at/harness/index.ts:181` and `:188`), whose witness always returns `stand-in` (`tests/at/harness/capabilities.ts:158`). The above-loop gate inspects the whole ledger (`tests/at/harness/registry.ts:617`), while D3–D4 provide no live email capability or integration-tier omission.
    unverified-runtime-claim: no

[4] high: unexecutable    loop/items/AI4DEV-81/plan.md:88
    claim: The attested clock specified by D4 cannot satisfy the harness types because it deliberately lacks the methods the contract requires.
    why it matters: `AtHarness.clock` must implement `freezeAt` and `advance` (`tests/at/harness/contracts.ts:54`), adapter construction currently requires `ControlledClock` (`tests/at/harness/index.ts:41`), and the ledger always constructs one (`tests/at/harness/index.ts:175`). Supplying the planned no-seam clock therefore fails typechecking unless the plan adds a tier-aware contract and adapter redesign; retaining or casting the seam contradicts D4’s provenance claim.
    unverified-runtime-claim: no

[5] critical: false-green    loop/items/AI4DEV-81/plan.md:76
    claim: D3 assigns provenance at the wrong granularity because every REQ-001 id shares the single `sut.accounts` key.
    why it matters: the suite binds only `accounts` (`tests/at/suites/req-001/_bind.ts:34`), while that object contains all live, unbuilt, and Discovery methods. Marking `sut.accounts` real because some methods use the slot can hide nested stand-ins from D2; marking it pending makes every floor id red. The promised “back a subset of sut keys and proxy every other key” cannot represent this suite without method-level provenance and a new callable pending mechanism.
    unverified-runtime-claim: no

[6] critical: shared-state risk    loop/items/AI4DEV-81/plan.md:155
    claim: The transient `jwt_expiry` step has neither a safe slot-management mechanism nor an oracle capable of proving restoration.
    why it matters: the test child receives connection values but no slot directory or pool authority, while `prepare()` owns configuration mirroring, restarts, and the configuration-hash marker (`tests/at/harness/db-pool.ts:1189`). Editing the repository file cannot affect the already-started slot, and `git diff supabase/config.toml` cannot observe the external slot copy; editing or restarting the slot directly can leave Auth running with the lowered expiry while the unchanged marker prevents the next preparation from restarting it.
    unverified-runtime-claim: no

[7] critical: false-green    loop/items/AI4DEV-81/plan.md:183
